# {{PROJECT_NAME}} Coding Rules Log

This log turns repeated manual corrections into durable, reviewable engineering
rules. It is evidence, not automatic write authorization.

## Promotion threshold

Create a candidate when substantially the same manual edit or review correction:

- appears in at least three locations in the inspected scope; or
- recurs across separate approved tasks, reviews, or regressions.

Do not infer recurrence without observable diffs, review records, tests, or an
owner-provided example.

## Candidate record

For each candidate, record:

| Field | Required content |
| --- | --- |
| ID | Stable `RULE-CANDIDATE-NNN` identifier |
| Status | observed / proposed / approved / enforced / rejected / superseded |
| Evidence | Exact files, reviews, tasks, or test failures showing recurrence |
| Scope | Applicable languages, modules, generated/manual code, and exclusions |
| Problem | Why the repeated form harms readability, correctness, or maintenance |
| Replacement | The preferred code shape or rule, with a concise example |
| Conflicts | Existing rules, tools, contracts, or compatibility constraints |
| Enforcement | Prose, formatter, linter, static check, template, generator, or test |
| Migration | Future-only, touched-code, or separately approved historical migration |
| Decision | Approver, approval reference, date, and reason |

## Active candidates

No recurring correction has been verified yet.

## Promotion workflow

1. Confirm the recurrence and root cause; do not promote personal taste from one example.
2. Compare the candidate with the selected language authority and verified project rules.
3. Propose the exact standards, tool, test, template, and migration files affected.
4. Apply `GATE-01` or `GATE-02` before changing a rule or enforcement configuration.
5. Replace obsolete active guidance, update affected verification, and mark the
   candidate `approved`, `enforced`, `rejected`, or `superseded` with evidence.

Prefer automatic enforcement for mechanical rules. Keep architecture, design,
ownership, and decomposition judgment in prose plus code review when reliable
mechanical enforcement would create false positives.
